\subsection*{7.4 Summary}

In this chapter, you have studied the following points :
1. The distance between \(\mathrm{P}\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Q}\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\right)\) is \(\sqrt{\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)^{2}+\left(y_{2}-y_{1}\right)^{2}}\).
2. The distance of a point \(\mathrm{P}(x, y)\) from the origin is \(\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\).
3. The coordinates of the point \(\mathrm{P}(x, y)\) which divides the line segment joining the points \(\mathrm{A}\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{B}\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\right)\) internally in the ratio \(m_{1}: m_{2}\) are \(\left(\frac{m_{1} x_{2}+m_{2} x_{1}}{m_{1}+m_{2}}, \frac{m_{1} y_{2}+m_{2} y_{1}}{m_{1}+m_{2}}\right)\).
4. The mid-point of the line segment joining the points \(\mathrm{P}\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Q}\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\right)\) is \(\left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}}{2}, \frac{y_{1}+y_{2}}{2}\right)\).

