\subsection*{8.3 Trigonometric Ratios of Some Specific Angles}

From geometry, you are already familiar with the construction of angles of \(30^{\circ}, 45^{\circ}\), \(60^{\circ}\) and \(90^{\circ}\). In this section, we will find the values of the trigonometric ratios for these angles and, of course, for \(0^{\circ}\).

\section*{Trigonometric Ratios of \(\mathbf{4 5}^{\circ}\)}

In \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\), right-angled at B , if one angle is \(45^{\circ}\), then the other angle is also \(45^{\circ}\), i.e., \(\angle \mathrm{A}=\angle \mathrm{C}=45^{\circ}\) (see Fig. 8.14).
So,
\[\mathrm{BC}=\mathrm{AB} \quad(\text { Why? })\]

Now, Suppose \(\mathrm{BC}=\mathrm{AB}=a\).

Fig. 8.14

Then by Pythagoras Theorem, \(\mathrm{AC}^{2}=\mathrm{AB}^{2}+\mathrm{BC}^{2}=a^{2}+a^{2}=2 a^{2}\), and, therefore, \(\quad \mathrm{AC}=a \sqrt{2}\).

Using the definitions of the trigonometric ratios, we have :
\[\begin{aligned}
& \sin 45^{\circ}=\frac{\text { side opposite to angle } 45^{\circ}}{\text { hypotenuse }}=\frac{\mathrm{BC}}{\mathrm{AC}}=\frac{a}{a \sqrt{2}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
& \cos 45^{\circ}=\frac{\text { side adjacent to angle } 45^{\circ}}{\text { hypotenuse }}=\frac{\mathrm{AB}}{\mathrm{AC}}=\frac{a}{a \sqrt{2}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
& \tan 45^{\circ}=\frac{\text { side opposite to angle } 45^{\circ}}{\text { side adjacent to angle } 45^{\circ}}=\frac{\mathrm{BC}}{\mathrm{AB}}=\frac{a}{a}=1
\end{aligned}\]

Also, \(\operatorname{cosec} 45^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\sin 45^{\circ}}=\sqrt{2}, \sec 45^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\cos 45^{\circ}}=\sqrt{2}, \cot 45^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\tan 45^{\circ}}=1\).

\section*{Trigonometric Ratios of \(\mathbf{3 0}^{\boldsymbol{\circ}}\) and \(\mathbf{6 0}^{\boldsymbol{\circ}}\)}

Let us now calculate the trigonometric ratios of \(30^{\circ}\) and \(60^{\circ}\). Consider an equilateral triangle ABC . Since each angle in an equilateral triangle is \(60^{\circ}\), therefore, \(\angle \mathrm{A}=\angle \mathrm{B}=\angle \mathrm{C}=60^{\circ}\).
Draw the perpendicular AD from A to the side BC (see Fig. 8.15).
Now
\[\triangle \mathrm{ABD} \cong \triangle \mathrm{ACD} \quad \text { (Why?) }\]

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/0f6def98-c5f4-41d3-8cce-ab69b5f89494-168.jpg?height=294&width=326&top_left_y=1519&top_left_x=1083}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 8.15}
\end{figure}

Therefore, and
\[\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{BD} & =\mathrm{DC} \\
\angle \mathrm{BAD} & =\angle \mathrm{CAD}(\mathrm{CPCT})
\end{aligned}\]

Now observe that:
\(\triangle \mathrm{ABD}\) is a right triangle, right-angled at D with \(\angle \mathrm{BAD}=30^{\circ}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{ABD}=60^{\circ}\) (see Fig. 8.15).

As you know, for finding the trigonometric ratios, we need to know the lengths of the sides of the triangle. So, let us suppose that \(\mathrm{AB}=2 a\).

Then,
\[\mathrm{BD}=\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{BC}=a\]
and
\[\mathrm{AD}^{2}=\mathrm{AB}^{2}-\mathrm{BD}^{2}=(2 a)^{2}-(a)^{2}=3 a^{2}\]

Therefore,
\[\mathrm{AD}=a \sqrt{3}\]

Now, we have :
\[\begin{aligned}
& \sin 30^{\circ}=\frac{\mathrm{BD}}{\mathrm{AB}}=\frac{a}{2 a}=\frac{1}{2}, \cos 30^{\circ}=\frac{\mathrm{AD}}{\mathrm{AB}}=\frac{a \sqrt{3}}{2 a}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \\
& \tan 30^{\circ}=\frac{\mathrm{BD}}{\mathrm{AD}}=\frac{a}{a \sqrt{3}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
\end{aligned}\]

Also, \(\quad \operatorname{cosec} 30^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\sin 30^{\circ}}=2, \quad \sec 30^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\cos 30^{\circ}}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\)
\[\cot 30^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\tan 30^{\circ}}=\sqrt{3}\]

Similarly,
\[\begin{aligned}
\sin 60^{\circ} & =\frac{\mathrm{AD}}{\mathrm{AB}}=\frac{a \sqrt{3}}{2 a}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, \cos 60^{\circ}=\frac{1}{2}, \tan 60^{\circ}=\sqrt{3}, \\
\operatorname{cosec} 60^{\circ} & =\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}, \text { sec } 60^{\circ}=2 \text { and } \cot 60^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} .
\end{aligned}\]

\section*{Trigonometric Ratios of \(\mathbf{0}^{\boldsymbol{\circ}}\) and \(\mathbf{9 0}^{\boldsymbol{\circ}}\)}

Let us see what happens to the trigonometric ratios of angle A, if it is made smaller and smaller in the right triangle ABC (see Fig. 8.16), till it becomes zero. As \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) gets smaller and smaller, the length of the side BC decreases. The point C gets closer to point B , and finally when \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) becomes very close to \(0^{\circ}, \mathrm{AC}\) becomes almost the same as AB (see Fig. 8.17).

Fig. 8.16

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/0f6def98-c5f4-41d3-8cce-ab69b5f89494-169.jpg?height=236&width=1339&top_left_y=1861&top_left_x=150}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig. 8.17}
\end{figure}

When \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) is very close to \(0^{\circ}, \mathrm{BC}\) gets very close to 0 and so the value of \(\sin \mathrm{A}=\frac{\mathrm{BC}}{\mathrm{AC}}\) is very close to 0 . Also, when \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) is very close to \(0^{\circ}, \mathrm{AC}\) is nearly the same as \(A B\) and so the value of \(\cos A=\frac{A B}{A C}\) is very close to 1 .

This helps us to see how we can define the values of \(\sin \mathrm{A}\) and \(\cos \mathrm{A}\) when \(\mathrm{A}=0^{\circ}\). We define : \(\boldsymbol{\operatorname { s i n }} \mathbf{0}^{\circ}=\mathbf{0}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\operatorname { c o s }} \mathbf{0}^{\circ}=\mathbf{1}\).

Using these, we have :
\[\tan 0^{\circ}=\frac{\sin 0^{\circ}}{\cos 0^{\circ}}=0, \cot 0^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\tan 0^{\circ}}, \text { which is not defined. (Why?) }\]
sec \(0^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\cos 0^{\circ}}=1\) and \(\operatorname{cosec} 0^{\circ}=\frac{1}{\sin 0^{\circ}}\), which is again not defined.(Why?)
Now, let us see what happens to the trigonometric ratios of \(\angle \mathrm{A}\), when it is made larger and larger in \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) till it becomes \(90^{\circ}\). As \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) gets larger and larger, \(\angle \mathrm{C}\) gets smaller and smaller. Therefore, as in the case above, the length of the side AB goes on decreasing. The point A gets closer to point B . Finally when \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) is very close to \(90^{\circ}\), \(\angle \mathrm{C}\) becomes very close to \(0^{\circ}\) and the side AC almost coincides with side BC (see Fig. 8.18).

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/0f6def98-c5f4-41d3-8cce-ab69b5f89494-170.jpg?height=240&width=1314&top_left_y=1399&top_left_x=154}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
