Determine \(\angle \mathrm{QPR}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{PRQ}\).
Solution : Given \(\mathrm{PQ}=3 \mathrm{~cm}\) and \(\mathrm{PR}=6 \mathrm{~cm}\).
Therefore, \(\quad \frac{\mathrm{PQ}}{\mathrm{PR}}=\sin \mathrm{R}\)

Fig. 8.20
or
\(\sin \mathrm{R}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac{1}{2}\)
So, \(\angle \mathrm{PRQ}=30^{\circ}\)
and therefore, \(\quad \angle \mathrm{QPR}=60^{\circ}\). (Why?)
You may note that if one of the sides and any other part (either an acute angle or any side) of a right triangle is known, the remaining sides and angles of the triangle can be determined.
Example 8 : If \(\sin (\mathrm{A}-\mathrm{B})=\frac{1}{2}, \cos (\mathrm{~A}+\mathrm{B})=\frac{1}{2}, 0^{\circ}<\mathrm{A}+\mathrm{B} \leq 90^{\circ}, \mathrm{A}>\mathrm{B}\), find A and B .

Solution : Since, \(\sin (A-B)=\frac{1}{2}\), therefore, \(A-B=30^{\circ} \quad\) (Why?)

Also, since \(\cos (A+B)=\frac{1}{2}\), therefore, \(A+B=60^{\circ}\) (Why?)

Solving (1) and (2), we get : \(\mathrm{A}=45^{\circ}\) and \(\mathrm{B}=15^{\circ}\).

